The general eligibility criteria for **CAP/DA services under NC Medicaid** are:

- **The person must be enrolled in NC Medicaid.** The policy states an eligible beneficiary must be enrolled in the NC Medicaid Program, and a person’s eligibility category may include restrictions that make them ineligible for CAP/DA services. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 2.1.1, effective 2025-09-01]

- **The requested CAP/DA service must meet general Medicaid coverage standards.** It must be:
  - individualized, specific, and consistent with the beneficiary’s symptoms or confirmed diagnosis, and not more than the person needs;
  - safely furnished, with no equally effective and more conservative or less costly statewide alternative available; and
  - not provided primarily for the convenience of the beneficiary, caregiver, or provider. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.1, effective 2025-09-01]

- **The person must meet CAP/DA-specific criteria.** NC Medicaid covers CAP/DA when the beneficiary:
  - **meets the required HCBS nursing facility level of care**, as determined by NC Medicaid, and needs long-term supports at a level typically provided in an institution, related to a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need;
  - has a **completed CAP assessment** showing a reasonable indication that the person will need at least one CAP/DA HCBS service within 30 calendar days of evaluation;
  - has a **signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services on a monthly basis to prevent institutionalization through coordinated case management;
  - or, for certain short-term equipment/modification/technology needs, has a signed service plan authorizing those items to help the person return to or remain in a primary private residence and prevent institutional placement;
  - is **able to maintain health, safety, and well-being** in the primary private residence or another approved location using formal and informal supports;
  - has **paid care needs that can be maintained within the average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
  - has a **primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
  - has an **emergency backup and disaster plan** identifying who will provide care if key staff or the primary caregiver cannot. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.2.1, effective 2025-09-01]

- **Age/scope context:** the policy’s case-management entity requirements refer to service capacity for **adults 18 years of age and older**, which is consistent with CAP/DA being the waiver for disabled adults. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 6.4.1, effective 2025-09-01]

## In short
A person generally must be an **NC Medicaid beneficiary**, meet **general medical-necessity/coverage standards**, and satisfy **CAP/DA waiver criteria** showing institutional-level need, a documented need for waiver services, an approved service plan, safe community placement, cost-effectiveness, physician coordination, and backup planning. The current policy I relied on is **Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, effective September 1, 2025**, and the amendment check returned that this document is the authoritative current version.

If you want, I can also give you:
1. a **plain-English checklist** for screening a client for CAP/DA, or  
2. the **reasons someone can be found ineligible or denied** under this policy.

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must have a **treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapy discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved** within the duration of the plan;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the plan.  
  [[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment), § 3.2.1.6, effective 2/1/2025]

Separately, for documentation, the provider record must include **a copy of the treatment plan** along with the other service documentation required by the policy. [[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment), § 7.2, effective 2/1/2025]

I checked amendments for Policy 10A, and the authoritative current document is the **2/1/2025** version of **Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to an identified beneficiary need and included in the person-centered service planning process. The beneficiary agrees to work with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for “equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services.” Covered equipment/modification/technology services must be used as listed in the Plan of Care, and the need must be reflected in the service plan. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)] (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current per amendment check)

## Requirements for home modifications under CAP/DA

CAP/DA covers home modifications in a beneficiary’s:

- primary private residence owned by the beneficiary or family;
- rented residence if the modifications are portable; or
- rented residence if the owner allows the modifications. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

Examples of covered home modifications include:

- ramps, grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- emergency egress when medically necessary due to physical limitations of the responsible party;
- bathroom modifications for accessibility, including toilet, shower/tub, sink fixtures, plumbing changes, and turnaround space;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- door handle and door modifications;
- non-skid surfaces;
- lifts and lift systems used primarily inside the home;
- certain smart home devices when the beneficiary lives alone and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan;
- certain air filtration systems and backup generators when specific medical conditions are met. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

More generally, equipment, modification, and technology services must serve one of these purposes:

- improve, maximize, or enhance mobility, safety, or independence;
- improve community integration;
- improve environmental and community accessibility; or
- address 24-hours-a-day, 7-days-a-week coverage concerns. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

For a requested home modification, the policy requires documentation including:

- **A recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested home modification;
- **The cost** of the item/modification required by the beneficiary;
- **A training plan** for the beneficiary and family on use of the equipment, if applicable; the case manager must document completion of the training, signed by the beneficiary or responsible party;
- **A statement of need** identified in the service plan;
- **Evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- **Itemized shipping costs** in the proposal request;
- **Other information required for the specific equipment/modification requested**;
- **Quotes**, when required for purchase, adaptation, or modification, in the number required by NC Medicaid; and
- **Written recommendations from appropriate professionals** when NC Medicaid or its designee determines special circumstances require them. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

The policy also lists supporting documentation that must be maintained/provided for equipment, supplies, adaptations, or modifications:

- a **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested modification;
- the **estimated life of the equipment** and the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit, if applicable; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date the equipment/adaptation/modification was provided, the cost, and related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Bottom line

For CAP/DA home modifications, the MDT must document that the modification is needed for the beneficiary’s safety, independence, accessibility, community integration, or round-the-clock care needs, and that the need is reflected in the service plan/Plan of Care. The record should also include the MDT recommendation, declaration of need assessment, medical/physician support when applicable, cost information, required quotes, training plan/documentation, and supplier invoice.  

If you want, I can also pull together the **main exclusions/limits for home modifications under CAP/DA** into a short checklist.

Physicians should provide Medicaid fluoride varnish services **from tooth eruption until the beneficiary reaches 3½ years of age**. The related oral screening/oral evaluation service is also **limited to beneficiaries under 3½ years of age**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

## Oral screening requirements

North Carolina Medicaid’s current **Physician Fluoride Varnish Services** policy, effective **Dec. 4, 2019**, requires the oral screening package to include:

- **Early caries screening** and detection of notable findings in the mouth using **a dental mirror and directed light**;
- **Counseling and educational materials** on good oral hygiene practices and nutrition for children;
- **Prescribing a fluoride supplement, if indicated**;
- **Application of fluoride varnish to all erupted primary teeth**, beginning at tooth eruption until age **3½**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

## Documentation required in the health record

The beneficiary’s record must document all of the following:

- **An oral evaluation and any notable findings**;
- **Preventive oral health and dietary counseling** with the primary caregiver;
- **Application of fluoride varnish**; and
- **Referral to a dentist, if appropriate**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

## Billing-related limits tied to the screening

The policy also states:

- Oral evaluation code **D0145** must be billed **with** fluoride varnish code **D1206**;
- Both are limited to beneficiaries **under 3½ years of age**;
- They are allowed **once every 60 calendar days**; and
- They are limited to **six times before the beneficiary reaches 3½ years of age**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

I checked amendments for Policy **1A-23** and found no listed change notices in the corpus; the authoritative document returned was **Physician Fluoride Varnish Services** itself, effective **Dec. 4, 2019**.

Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet both **initial/ongoing certification standards** and can be **decertified** for specific compliance, staffing, documentation, billing, and operational failures.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid policy 12B requires an HIV case management provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of 3 years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are delivered by **qualified case managers**;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors**;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- comply with **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records** and inspect agency operations and financial records;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed ownership/name/address/telephone/business-dissolution changes **within 30 calendar days of the proposed change** and **no later than 5 business days after the actual change**; and
- achieve **national accreditation within 1 year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting bodies: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (effective 2020-01-15), § 6.2.1.

The policy also reflects additional certification application/operational requirements, including:

- a **physical business site** verified by site visit, not in a private residence or vehicle;
- organizational and ownership disclosures;
- required business planning and financial information;
- computer capability for Medicaid IT, HIPAA security, electronic billing, EFT, and required communications;
- completion of a **precertification onsite visit**;
- compliance with all applicable licensure/certification requirements; and
- written policies on confidentiality, grievances, beneficiary rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflict of interest, electronic and medical records, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (effective 2020-01-15), administrative requirements section.

The provider must also maintain a **quality improvement plan** addressing health outcomes, monitoring/evaluation of records, complaints, statistical and utilization studies, claims/service-record accuracy, and staff/provider qualification compliance. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (effective 2020-01-15), case management requirements section.

For **recertification**, the NC Medicaid checklist calls for, among other items:

- one application per physical location;
- a signed recertification checklist;
- listing all case managers and supervisors with employment dates;
- listing all counties served and services provided;
- operating hours and emergency after-hours plan;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- satisfaction-survey frequency and how results are used; and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf).

## Grounds for decertification

A provider may be decertified for any of the following reasons:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a required **written plan of correction** within **30 calendar days** for unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet policy qualifications;
- failure of staff to complete required **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units (CEUs)**;
- failure to provide staff supervision adequate to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within required time frames;
- failure to provide documentation sufficient to support billing, including documentation required by policy;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of agency name, director, ownership, address, or telephone changes, causing inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal/state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in § 6.2. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (effective 2020-01-15), § 6.2.5.

## Practical takeaway

In short, HIV case management providers must show:
- organizational stability and prior HIV case-management experience,
- qualified staff and supervision,
- Medicaid enrollment for each site,
- accreditation,
- compliance systems, documentation, and quality improvement,
- and ongoing operational reporting to NC Medicaid.

They can lose certification not only for fraud or serious misconduct, but also for **documentation failures, staffing/training deficiencies, missed corrective-action deadlines, failure to maintain certification conditions, or failure to actually enroll beneficiaries after certification**.

I checked amendment status for both Policy 12B and the recertification checklist, and both were returned as the authoritative current documents with no listed amendments in the corpus.
